0	102	The Santer Commission was the European Commission in office between 23 January 1995 and 15 March 1999.
103	187	The administration was led by Jacques Santer (former Prime Minister of Luxembourg).

187	252	The body had 20 members and oversaw the introduction of the euro.
253	362	It was cut short when the Commission became the first to resign en masse, owing to allegations of corruption.
363	449	Some members continued under Manuel Marín until the Prodi Commission was appointed.



449	571	Appointment
In 1994, Jacques Delors was due to step down from a successful tenure as President of the European Commission.
572	653	However, his federalist style was not to the liking of many national governments.
654	813	Hence, when Jean-Luc Dehaene (the then Prime Minister of Belgium) was nominated as his successor, he was vetoed by the UK on the grounds he was too federalist.
814	955	Jacques Santer, then-Prime Minister of Luxembourg, was seen as less federalist, for his presidency had earlier proposed the pillar structure.
956	1034	Hence, he was nominated and approved by the European Council on 15 July 1994.

1034	1165	Being seen as the "second choice" weakened Santer's position, with the European Parliament approving him only by a narrow majority.
1166	1289	He himself admitted that he "was not the first choice – but to become Commission president was not my first choice either."
1290	1372	He did, however, flex his powers over the nominations for the other Commissioners.
1373	1472	The President gained this power under the Maastricht Treaty that came into force the previous year.
1473	1648	On 18 January 1995, he got his Commission approved by Parliament by 416 votes to 103 (a larger majority than expected) and they were appointed by the council on 23 January.



